<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Analytic Capacity and Rational Approximation</title>

  <p>
    The material in this section is not directly related to the project, but illustrates another motivation for studying analytic capacity
  </p>

  <p>
    It can be difficult to perform certain calculations, such as computing the derivative, with analytic functions. For this reason we seek to approximate a given analytic function by a sequence of simpler functions, which are much easier to work with, so that we can replace the calculation with the analytic function with the calculation with a simpler function and obtain an answer which is almost the same as the exact answer. As it turns out, rational functions work well for this purpose.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-runge">
    <title>Runge's Theorem</title>
    
    
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>K</m> be a compact subset of <m>\mathbb C</m> and suppose <m>f</m> is analytic on <m>K</m>. If <m>A</m> is a set containing at least one complex number from each connected component of <m>\hat{\mathbb C} \setminus K</m> then there exists a sequence <m>\{f_n\}</m> of rational functions that converges uniformly to <m>f</m> in <m>K</m> and whose poles are points in <m>A</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <definition xml:id="def-uniform-convergence">
    <title>Uniform Convergence</title>
    
    
    <statement>
        <p>
            A sequence <m>\{f_n\}</m> is said to <term>converge uniformly</term> to a function <m>f</m> on <m>K</m> if for every <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m> there exists <m>N \in \mathbb N</m> such that whenever <m>n &gt; N</m>,
            <md>
                |f_n(z)-f(z)| &lt; \epsilon
            </md>
            for all <m>z \in K</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
<p>
  Let <m>X</m> be a compact subset of <m>\mathbb C</m>. An important question in the theory of rational approximation is to determine when every continuous function on <m>X</m> can be uniformly approximated by rational functions with poles off <m>X</m>. We can restate this question in the language of function spaces. 
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-RX">
  <title>R(X)</title>
  
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>X</m> be a compact subset of <m>\mathbb C</m> and let <m>R_0(X)</m> denote the set of rational functions with poles outside <m>X</m>. The function space <m>R(X)</m> is the uniform closure of <m>R_0(X)</m>. This means that <m>R(X)</m> consists of all functions that can be uniformly approximated by rational functions with poles off <m>X</m>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<definition xml:id="def-CX">
  <title>C(X)</title>
  
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>X</m> be a compact subset of <m>\mathbb C</m>. <m>C(X)</m> denotes the space of functions that are continuous on <m>X</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<p>
  Thus the previous question can be rephrased as follows. When does <m>R(X) = C(X)</m>? This question was answered by Anatoli Vitushkin using analytic capacity. 
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-vitushkin">
  <title>Vitushkin's Theorem</title>
  
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      The following are equivalent for a compact set <m>X</m>:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>R(X) = C(X)</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For every open set <m>D</m>, <m>\gamma(D \setminus X) = \gamma(D)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For <m>dxdy</m>-almost all <m>z\in X</m>, it is true that 
            <md>
              \limsup_{r\to 0} \frac{\gamma(B(z,r)\setminus X)}{r} &gt; 0
            </md>
            
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<p>
  Another use of analytic capacity occurs in the characterization of bounded point derivations. 
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-pd">
  <title> Point Derivation</title>
  
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      A <term>point derivation</term> on <m>R(X)</m> is a mapping <m>D: R(X) \to \mathbb C</m> defined by <m>D(f) = f'(z_0)</m> where <m>z_0</m> is a fixed point in <m>X</m>. 
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<definition xml:id="def-bpd">
  <title>Bounded Point Derivation</title>
  
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      <m>R(X)</m> admits a <term>bounded point derivation</term> at <m>z_0</m> if there exists a positive constant <m>K</m> such that
      <md>
        |f'(z_0)| \leq K \sup_{z \in X} |f(z)|
      </md>
      
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<p>
  Bounded point derivations are studied with respect to approximation with derivatives. The existence of a bounded point derivation shows that the rational approximation extends to derivatives; that is, the sequence of derivatives of the rational functions converges uniformly to the derivative of the limit function.
</p>

<p>
  Alfred Hallstrom proved a theorem characterizing bounded point derivations in terms of analytic capacity.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-hallstrom">
  <title>Hallstrom's Theorem</title>
  
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>A_n(z_0)</m> denote the annulus centered at <m>z_0</m> with inner radius <m>2^{-(n+1)}</m> and outer radius <m>2^{-n}</m>. That is <m>A_n(z_0) = \{z \in \mathbb C: 2^{-(n+1)} \leq |z-z_0| \leq 2^{-n}\}</m>. Then <m>R(X)</m> admits a bounded point derivation at <m>z_0</m> if and only if the series
      <md>
        \sum_{n=1}^\infty 4^n \gamma(A_n(z_0)\setminus X)
      </md>
      converges.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<p>
  Question to Ponder: Create a set <m>X</m> by starting with the unit disk and removing a disk with radius <m>4^{-n} \cdot n^{-2}</m> from the annulus <m>A_n(0)</m> for <m>n = 1,2,3, \ldots</m>. Such a set is called a <term>roadrunner set</term>. Call this set <m>X</m> and show that <m>R(X)</m> admits a bounded point derivation at <m>0</m> by showing that the series in Hallstrom's Theorem converges.
</p>



  </section>